body='''<h1>Did the learning app help?</h1>
<section class="audit" aria-labelledby="sources"><h2 id="sources">Source audit</h2>
<h3><a href="https://miguelhernan.org/whatifbook">Causal Inference: What If</a></h3>
<p class="meta"><em>Miguel A. Hernán and James M. Robins · methods textbook · Chapman &amp; Hall/CRC citation, 2020; online version January 2, 2025</em></p>
<p>These Harvard researchers develop methods for learning the effects of interventions from experiments and observational data. Chapters 1–3 explain potential outcomes, random assignment, and the assumptions behind nonrandom comparisons; Chapter 10 addresses uncertainty. Those logical foundations apply to education too; the book’s medical examples are not evidence about this app. Their expertise is documented by <a href="https://hsph.harvard.edu/research/causalab/our-history/">Harvard’s research group</a>.</p>
<h3><a href="https://www.stats.ox.ac.uk/~evans/APTS/intro.html">Causal Inference — Chapter 1: Introduction</a></h3>
<p class="meta"><em>Vanessa Didelez and Robin J. Evans · university course notes · University of Oxford hosted APTS materials · November 26, 2025</em></p>
<p>The authors teach causal inference through the Advanced Postgraduate Training in Statistics programme. Sections 1.1–1.3 explain why a causal question must specify an intervention, comparison, population, outcome, and time. I use that framework to make “helped” testable. These are methods notes, not an app evaluation.</p>
<h3><a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/2332858416673968">Online Mathematics Homework Increases Student Achievement</a></h3>
<p class="meta"><em>Jeremy Roschelle, Mingyu Feng, Robert F. Murphy, and Craig A. Mason · randomized field study · AERA Open · October 23, 2016</em></p>
<p>This team conducted the school experiment described below. Their firsthand report documents assignment, testing, and results for ASSISTments with teacher support. I use its original Figure 2 and the <a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/2332858417707489">April 17, 2017 correction</a> to Table 4. The study shows how a causal evaluation works; its age and specific setting prevent treating it as a verdict on today’s unspecified app.</p>
<h3><a href="https://ies.ed.gov/ncee/wwc/Study/86375">Online Mathematics Homework Increases Student Achievement — Study Review</a></h3>
<p class="meta"><em>What Works Clearinghouse · independent evidence review · U.S. Institute of Education Sciences · August 2026 review</em></p>
<p>This federal evidence-review programme evaluates studies against published research standards. Its latest review rates this study as meeting standards without reservations and reports the adjusted score comparison. That supports confidence in this particular design, not every product or setting. The <a href="https://ies.ed.gov/use-work/awards/efficacy-study-online-mathematics-homework-support-evaluation-assistments-formative-assessment-and">funding agency’s project record</a> also documents the teacher training, school setting, and laptop access.</p></section>
<section><h2>The direct answer</h2>
<p class="lead">App users may have scored higher even if the app did nothing. To learn whether it helped, we need a credible estimate of how those students would have scored without it.</p>
<p><strong>Causal inference</strong> means using evidence and explicit assumptions to estimate what would change if we changed an action. A higher average among users is an <strong>association</strong>: two observed things occur together. The missing step is showing that changing app use changes the result.</p>
<h2>1. “Helped” compares two possible outcomes</h2>
<p>Suppose Maya used the app and scored 84 out of 100. Her score alone cannot tell us the effect. If she would have scored 74 without the app, it helped by 10 points. If she would have scored 84 anyway, it helped by zero. If she would have scored 90, it reduced her score by 6 points. All three possibilities fit the one observed score.</p>
<p>The unobserved alternative is a <strong>counterfactual</strong>. We cannot observe Maya both using and not using the app during the same study period. Testing her before and after does not recover that alternative: she also attends classes, practices, and grows familiar with exams. An earlier score answers a different question. This distinction follows <a href="https://miguelhernan.org/s/hernanrobins_WhatIf_2jan25.pdf#page=12">What If, §§1.1–1.2</a>.</p>
<p>We can often estimate an <strong>average effect</strong> across students without discovering each person’s effect. First define the comparison. For example: “For this school’s seventh graders, what is the average difference on the same end-of-term exam if we offer eight weeks of app-based homework instead of the usual homework?” An offer, a required lesson, and voluntary heavy use are different interventions. So are adding practice and replacing practice. <a href="https://www.stats.ox.ac.uk/~evans/APTS/intro.html#target-trials">Course notes, §1.2</a>.</p>
<h2>2. Higher scores can come entirely from who uses it</h2>
<p>Students who already know more may be more likely to choose the app. Their prior knowledge also helps them on the exam. That makes prior knowledge a possible <strong>confounder</strong>: a preexisting factor affecting both app use and scores. Motivation, tutoring, and internet access could matter too. Their presence and direction are questions to investigate, not facts established by the score difference.</p>
<p>The following constructed example shows why even a large difference is insufficient. There are 200 students: 100 more prepared and 100 less prepared. Assume every more-prepared student scores 90 and every less-prepared student scores 60, with or without the app. The app’s effect is exactly zero by construction.</p>
<div class="experiment"><h3>Change who chooses the app</h3><p class="small">Original teaching model. All numbers are invented. Every group contains 100 students; scores are points out of 100.</p>
<label for="mix">More-prepared students among the 100 app users: <output id="mixVal">80</output></label>
<input id="mix" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="80" step="1">
<table><caption>Group composition and resulting averages</caption><thead><tr><th>Students</th><th>App users</th><th>Nonusers</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>More prepared: score 90</th><td id="appHigh">80</td><td id="noHigh">20</td></tr>
<tr><th>Less prepared: score 60</th><td id="appLow">20</td><td id="noLow">80</td></tr>
<tr><th>Average exam score</th><td id="appAvg">84.0</td><td id="noAvg">66.0</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p id="result" role="status" aria-live="polite">Observed difference: +18.0 points. Actual app effect: 0 points.</p>
<button id="reset" type="button">Reset to 80</button>
<noscript><p>The default example works without JavaScript. Interactive changes require JavaScript.</p></noscript></div>
<p>At the starting setting, users average (80 × 90 + 20 × 60) ÷ 100 = 84. Nonusers average 66. The entire 18-point advantage comes from having more prepared students among users. At 50 prepared students in each group, both averages become 75. Below 50, the app appears harmful even though its effect is still zero.</p>
<p>This is a mathematical demonstration of a possible explanation, not a diagnosis of the real app. An app could help and still have low-scoring users if struggling students seek it out. Bigger datasets make averages more stable; they do not remove systematic differences in who participates. <a href="https://miguelhernan.org/s/hernanrobins_WhatIf_2jan25.pdf#page=36">What If, Chapter 3</a>.</p>
<h2>3. Random assignment changes how the groups are formed</h2>
<p>Recruit students before deciding who gets access. Then let a random procedure assign them to app-based homework or usual homework. A student’s motivation, preparation, or parent cannot determine the assignment. Over repeated assignments, neither group systematically gets the students who would have scored higher anyway.</p>
<p>That is why the comparison becomes informative: the usual-homework group estimates what would have happened to the app-assigned group without the app. We still do not observe anyone’s missing outcome. We use a group formed by chance to estimate the missing average. Random assignment makes this credible in expectation; it does not guarantee two perfectly identical groups in a particular experiment. <a href="https://miguelhernan.org/s/hernanrobins_WhatIf_2jan25.pdf#page=22">What If, §2.1</a>.</p>
<p>For a concrete trial, agree on the following before results arrive:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Define the treatment and comparison.</strong> Specify app version, homework expectations, duration, and usual instruction. If extra tutoring accompanies the app, the trial tests that whole package.</li><li><strong>Assign by chance.</strong> Randomize students if they can receive separate conditions. Randomize classes or schools if teachers would otherwise mix the conditions or students would share access.</li><li><strong>Use a common outcome.</strong> Give both groups the same independently scored exam at the same time, and collect prior scores before assignment.</li><li><strong>Follow everyone assigned.</strong> Seek exam results even from students who stop using the app, and record missing results and crossover.</li><li><strong>Compare the assigned groups.</strong> Report the score difference, its uncertainty, and the smallest gain that would justify the time and cost.</li></ol>
<p>These steps are an application of the trial-definition framework and the school study’s methods. With classes assigned, many classmates share a teacher and learning environment. They are not as much independent evidence as the same number of separately assigned students. The analysis must account for that grouping.</p>
<h2>4. What an actual education experiment found</h2>
<p>Can this design produce evidence beyond a comparison of volunteers? In Maine, researchers assigned schools to ASSISTments with teacher professional development or usual homework. The final analysis covered 2,850 seventh graders in 43 schools. The end-of-year test was scored without the scorer knowing the assigned condition. See the study’s <a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/2332858416673968">Research Design and Measures sections</a>.</p>
<p>How large was the estimated average difference? The federal review reports these results:</p>
<table><caption>Published adjusted results, reformatted from the WWC Findings table</caption><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Average TerraNova scale score</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>ASSISTments package</th><td>690.79</td></tr><tr><th>Usual homework</th><td>681.95</td></tr><tr><th>Difference</th><td>+8.84 scale points</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p class="small"><a href="https://ies.ed.gov/ncee/wwc/Study/86375">WWC review, August 2026, Findings</a>. Full analyzed sample: 2,850 students. The article’s model accounts for prior achievement and other measured characteristics. These are test-scale points, not percentages or points out of 100.</p>
<p>The positive difference supports a benefit under this design. The justification comes from how schools were assigned and followed, not from 690.79 exceeding 681.95. The WWC judged the study to have acceptably low school attrition and student nonresponse. That is a quality judgment, not a guarantee that missing data have no effect.</p>
<p>Did the average conceal differences by starting achievement? The original figure compares students below and above the middle of the prior-score distribution:</p>
<figure><img class="source" src="data:image/png;base64,FIGURE" alt="Published bar chart: in both low and high prior-achievement groups, treatment students have higher observed TerraNova scores than controls; the gap is larger in the low group."><figcaption>Original Figure 2, Roschelle and colleagues (2016), p. 9. <a href="source-figure2.png">Open full-size figure</a>. CC BY 3.0; unchanged image extracted from the <a href="https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1194398.pdf#page=9">published PDF</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Compare dark and light bars <em>within</em> each starting-achievement group. The lower group’s observed means are 665.29 versus 650.94; the higher group’s are 717.80 versus 711.95. The <a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/2332858417707489">corrected Table 4</a> reports differences of 14.35 and 5.84 scale points. Rounding of the displayed higher-group means accounts for the last-decimal discrepancy.</p>
<p>Notice the vertical axis begins at 487, not zero. Bar lengths therefore cannot be read as proportional gains. These are unadjusted subgroup averages, whereas the previous table gives adjusted overall results. The chart suggests the average benefit differed by prior preparation; it cannot tell us which individual child benefited.</p>
<p>The intervention included teacher training and feedback reports, and students had school-provided laptops. It estimates that package’s effect in that context. It does not isolate a software feature, explain exactly how learning improved, or establish what a different app would do.</p>
<h2>5. A fair assignment still needs careful interpretation</h2>
<p><strong>Assignment is different from actual use.</strong> Imagine half the app-assigned students never open it. Keeping them in the app-assigned group estimates the effect of offering the programme as implemented. Comparing only enthusiastic users against controls reintroduces self-selection. Estimating the effect of actual use requires additional assumptions and analysis.</p>
<p><strong>Missing exams can change the comparison.</strong> Suppose discouraged app users skip the final exam. Averaging only those who remain could overstate success. Record how many results are missing in each group and examine how plausible missing scores would change the conclusion. Sharing app materials across groups can also change which intervention is being compared.</p>
<p><strong>Chance can produce a difference.</strong> A random assignment might happen to place stronger students in one group. An uncertainty interval expresses how precisely the experiment estimates the effect under its assumptions. A hypothetical estimate of +4 points with a 95% interval from −1 to +9 would leave both a small harm and a useful benefit compatible with the data. An interval from +3 to +5 would be more precise. These are examples, not results from the Maine study. A 95% procedure is designed to cover the true effect in 95% of repeated studies under its assumptions; it does not protect against a badly designed comparison. <a href="https://miguelhernan.org/s/hernanrobins_WhatIf_2jan25.pdf#page=141">What If, §10.1 and Fine Point 10.2</a>.</p>
<p>Finally, average improvement does not mean everyone improves. And assigning participants randomly does not mean the participants were selected randomly from all students. A successful trial can be convincing for the studied group while leaving uncertainty about other ages, subjects, schools, or app versions.</p>
<h2>6. If we cannot run an experiment</h2>
<p>Use the same clear question and collect information from before app use: prior scores, available study time, tutoring, and other plausible common causes. Compare users with nonusers who have similar starting characteristics. In the constructed table, comparing prepared students with prepared students and less-prepared students with less-prepared students reveals the zero difference.</p>
<p>For real students, that approach depends on stronger assumptions: the relevant differences were measured well enough; comparable users and nonusers exist; and the recorded use represents the intervention we care about. Matching prior scores cannot remove an unmeasured motivation difference. If every prepared student uses the app and no comparable student abstains, that comparison is missing altogether. <a href="https://miguelhernan.org/s/hernanrobins_WhatIf_2jan25.pdf#page=36">What If, §§3.1–3.2</a>.</p>
<p>Before-and-after improvement alone also leaves ordinary teaching and changing exams as explanations. A credible access lottery or carefully justified policy change can sometimes support a stronger nonrandom study, but the details must explain why the groups would otherwise have had comparable outcomes.</p>
<p><strong>The useful question is: “Why is this comparison a credible estimate of what would have happened without the app?”</strong> Higher scores start the investigation. A defensible comparison, careful follow-up, and honest uncertainty make the causal conclusion possible.</p></section>
'''.replace('FIGURE',fig)
css='''*{box-sizing:border-box}html{background:#f5f4f0;color:#101527}body{margin:0;font:400 17px/1.75 Inter,Arial,sans-serif}main{max-width:768px;margin:0 auto;padding:58px 24px 90px}h1,h2,h3{font-family:Sora,Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-.035em;line-height:1.25}h1{font-size:46px;margin:0 0 42px}h2{font-size:28px;margin:52px 0 20px}h3{font-size:19px;margin:27px 0 8px}p{margin:14px 0}a{color:#2f6c9e;text-decoration:underline;font-weight:500;text-underline-offset:3px;overflow-wrap:anywhere}a:hover,a:focus{color:#bf3030}a.external span{color:#bf3030;font-size:.8em;white-space:nowrap}.meta{font-size:14px;color:#646c77;margin:0 0 13px;line-height:1.6}.audit{border-bottom:1px solid #cbd0d3;padding-bottom:24px}.audit p:not(.meta){font-size:15px}.audit h2{margin-top:0;font-size:22px}.lead{font-size:23px;line-height:1.55;font-weight:500}.small,figcaption{font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;color:#646c77}strong{font-weight:500}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;margin:20px 0}caption{text-align:left;font-weight:500;margin-bottom:12px}th,td{text-align:left;padding:12px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid #cbd0d3;vertical-align:top}th{font-weight:500}thead{background:#fff}figure{margin:28px 0}img{display:block;max-width:100%;height:auto}img.source{width:620px;margin:0 auto 14px}.experiment{border:1px solid #cbd0d3;background:#fff;padding:24px;margin:28px 0}.experiment h3{margin-top:0}.experiment label{display:block;font-weight:500;margin:20px 0 8px}input[type=range]{width:100%;accent-color:#2f6c9e;height:28px}button{font:500 15px Inter,Arial,sans-serif;background:#fff;color:#101527;border:1px solid #646c77;padding:9px 14px;cursor:pointer}button:hover{background:#f5f4f0}:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #bf3030;outline-offset:4px}#result{font-weight:500;color:#46734d}li{padding-left:4px;margin:13px 0}ol{padding-left:24px}@media(max-width:550px){main{padding:34px 20px 60px}h1{font-size:35px}h2{font-size:25px}.experiment{padding:16px}th,td{padding:9px 6px;font-size:13px}.lead{font-size:21px}}@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){*{scroll-behavior:auto}}'''
js='''const mix=document.querySelector('#mix');const el=id=>document.getElementById(id);function update(){const n=Number(mix.value);el('mixVal').textContent=n;el('appHigh').textContent=n;el('noHigh').textContent=100-n;el('appLow').textContent=100-n;el('noLow').textContent=n;el('appAvg').textContent=(60+.3*n).toFixed(1);el('noAvg').textContent=(90-.3*n).toFixed(1);const d=.6*n-30;el('result').textContent=`Observed difference: ${d>0?'+':''}${d.toFixed(1)} points. Actual app effect: 0 points.`}mix.addEventListener('input',update);el('reset').addEventListener('click',()=>{mix.value=80;update()});'''
